Root problem: construction costs = labor and materials
Problem we can solve: formwork
For an average 2,100 sq ft home in Ohio
Traditional Formwork
14 days
avg $24,000 in Equipment
$8,499 - $10,561 in material costs *includes waste
$11,5886 - $14,826 in labor costs
Foundation 3D Printed Formwork
25 Labor hours  = 2.5 days /job
Equipment cost??
Material Cost: $7,345 - $9,182
Labor Costs: $2,549
Total traditional formwork costs: 20,085-25,387
Total foundation formwork: 10596 - 13582
Total savingsL $9489- $11,805
Kate Traditional formwork is a bottleneck for the housing industry for a single house is:
7-14 days of work  (digging, forming, pouring, setting, and curing)
4-6 workers who will cost between $11,499-$14,339
Bc hourly wages are $23.40 for construction laborers and $2919 for cement mason/finishers
2,030 (sqft) * 0.242 (labor hours/sqft) ≈ 491 labor-hours
Up to $25 million in equipment:
Economies of scale is why our target market is subdivisions, This market is already interested in reducing costs and increasing efficiency
50 of these homes in a suburban subdivision will take a combined 2000-4000 labor hours for a crew to complete from hole in the ground to shingles on roofs
Target market segment is residential development construction within the united states; we are entering as a competitor against 3D printed concrete and traditional concrete subcontractors
TAM represents US &quot;Cookie-Cutter&quot; Attached/Detached Subdivision Market
The SAM is Concrete Subcontracting for Subdivision Starts in Ohio.
Our SOM shows that in 5 years we will hold $9.6 million show much of the market we will ahe in 5 years with a flee of 3 concrete printers that can do an average print time of your max capacity is roughly 75 homes per year (allowing for weather and maintenance downtime).
In our discovery with housing developers, industrial professional contractors, and concrete subcontractors, we rrealized the opportunity is that current formwork processes for construction takes  $590,000 to $770,000 in direct labor for a standard 50-home subdivision. Concrete substractors must find ways to lower their bids and stabilize their labor needs in a market where labor now accounts for 40% to 60% of total foundation costs. With our 3d printer, the only physical labor required is mixing the concrete to be poured in the printer and laying reinforcement
Major risk factors housing market and slow adoption
Market opportunity:
Identify target market segment
Total addressable market
Market is a reasonable estimate
Clearly identified problem or pain point
